{
  "case_id": "2021-20-1472",
  "document_hash": "6921b6cce87fb1cd8353949e5e006ef3e6d4c13a0044d0d571cc36eeda15288d",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Melissa Arbus Sherry",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1472",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6921b6cce87fb1cd8353949e5e006ef3e6d4c13a0044d0d571cc36eeda15288d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6921b6cce87f-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This Court has repeatedly held that time limits are rarely jurisdictional and that if Congress wants to make them jurisdictional, it has to speak clearly. Section 6330(d)(1) does not have the needed clarity."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1472",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6921b6cce87fb1cd8353949e5e006ef3e6d4c13a0044d0d571cc36eeda15288d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6921b6cce87f-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Mr. Bond, under current practices, are -- is this -- are these time limits ever tolled for any reason?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1472",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6921b6cce87fb1cd8353949e5e006ef3e6d4c13a0044d0d571cc36eeda15288d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6921b6cce87f-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: So does that mean it's well established -- look, the obvious thing, just, in English -- I don't know about the last antecedent rule or so forth, but -- but just in ordinary English, it says here \"such matter.\" Okay?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1472",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6921b6cce87fb1cd8353949e5e006ef3e6d4c13a0044d0d571cc36eeda15288d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6921b6cce87f-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Suppose that (d)(1) were worded just slightly differently, and this is not the kind of language you usually see in a statute, but indulge the hypothetical. So suppose it said the person may within 30 days of a determination under this section petition the Tax Court for review of such determination and, by the way, the Tax Court shall have jurisdiction with respect to such matter. What would you say then?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Bond (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1472",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "6921b6cce87fb1cd8353949e5e006ef3e6d4c13a0044d0d571cc36eeda15288d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6921b6cce87f-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think, as the Court decides the case in front of you, I think the -- the answer is not to say, well, Congress used the word \"jurisdictional,\" but it didn't use \"if\" or \"where,\" or it didn't put \"jurisdiction\" in the right place in the sentence, or it used paren -- parentheses instead of a semicolon. I think that does a disservice to Congress and becomes a drafting instruction to Heritage Reporting Corporation"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1472",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "6921b6cce87fb1cd8353949e5e006ef3e6d4c13a0044d0d571cc36eeda15288d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6921b6cce87f-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But the point of a clear statement rule, to pick up on Justice Kagan's question, is when legislation's drafted, first of all, there are expert drafters who are involved in the process and who do know some of the background principles."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Bond",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1472",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "6921b6cce87fb1cd8353949e5e006ef3e6d4c13a0044d0d571cc36eeda15288d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6921b6cce87f-0003-0004",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 73,
        "start": 73
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the universe of cases in which a person could petition is, on average, 26,000 or so a year."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Amicus briefs",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1472",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "6921b6cce87fb1cd8353949e5e006ef3e6d4c13a0044d0d571cc36eeda15288d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6921b6cce87f-0003-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 82,
        "start": 82
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The amicus briefs are replete with examples of individuals who did not get their day in court because the Tax Court deemed this deadline to be jurisdictional and not subject to equitable tolling. Ms. Castillo's case is currently pending in the Second Circuit. It is a perfect example of why this Congress who passed this statute would not have wanted this to be the rare and harsh jurisdictional deadline."
    },
